Q: How to handle the ... parameter when programming with dplyr A: <p>As noted in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79976454/6461462">Konrad's answer</a>, you can use <code>rlang::enquos()</code> to capture the <code>...</code> arguments as a list of quosures, which you can then iterate over like any regular list. Each element can be injected back into dplyr verbs with <code>!!</code> and converted to a string name with <code>rlang::as_label()</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-r prettyprint-override"><code>library(dplyr)

myfunc &lt;- function(data, ...) {
  vars &lt;- rlang::enquos(...)

  for (var in vars) {
    nm &lt;- rlang::as_label(var)
    data &lt;- data |&gt;
      group_by(!!var) |&gt;
      mutate(!!paste0(nm, &quot;_mean&quot;) := mean(!!var))
  }
  data
}

iris |&gt; myfunc(Sepal.Width, Sepal.Length)

#&gt; # A tibble: 150 × 7
#&gt; # Groups:   Sepal.Length [35]
#&gt;    Sepal.Length Sepal.Width Petal.Length Petal.Width Species Sepal.Width_mean
#&gt;           &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;fct&gt;              &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt;  1          5.1         3.5          1.4         0.2 setosa               3.5
#&gt;  2          4.9         3            1.4         0.2 setosa               3  
#&gt;  3          4.7         3.2          1.3         0.2 setosa               3.2
#&gt;  4          4.6         3.1          1.5         0.2 setosa               3.1
#&gt;  5          5           3.6          1.4         0.2 setosa               3.6
#&gt;  6          5.4         3.9          1.7         0.4 setosa               3.9
#&gt;  7          4.6         3.4          1.4         0.3 setosa               3.4
#&gt;  8          5           3.4          1.5         0.2 setosa               3.4
#&gt;  9          4.4         2.9          1.4         0.2 setosa               2.9
#&gt; 10          4.9         3.1          1.5         0.1 setosa               3.1
#&gt; # ℹ 140 more rows
#&gt; # ℹ 1 more variable: Sepal.Length_mean &lt;dbl&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><sup>Created on 2026-07-07 with <a href="https://reprex.tidyverse.org" rel="nofollow noreferrer">reprex v2.1.1</a></sup></p>
<p>Here, <code>rlang::enquos(...)</code> captures all <code>...</code> arguments as unevaluated quosures, then iterating over the list gives you one quosure per variable. <code>!!var</code> injects the quosure back into dplyr verbs (<code>group_by</code>, <code>mutate</code>, etc.). <code>rlang::as_label(var)</code> extracts the variable name as a string for naming new columns. And finally <code>:=</code> is needed on the left-hand side of <code>mutate</code> when the column name is computed programmatically.</p>
<p>This is equivalent to your working version but allows bare unquoted variable names in the function call.</p>
<p>You could also use <code>purrr::reduce</code> and (ab)use <code>across</code> and its <code>.names</code> variable;</p>
<pre class="lang-r prettyprint-override"><code>myfunc &lt;- function(data, ...) {
  vars &lt;- rlang::enquos(...)

  purrr::reduce(vars, function(data, var) {
    data |&gt;
      group_by(!!var) |&gt;
      mutate(across(!!var, mean, .names = &quot;{.col}_mean&quot;))
  }, .init = data)
}
</code></pre>

Q: warning: taking address of packed member of &#39;struct details&#39; may result in an unaligned pointer value [-Waddress-of-packed-member] A: <p>You can generate a helper type which encapsulates the unaligned type and give it to <code>print_status_details()</code>.  Compiler knows then about the unaligned data and generates correct code.</p>
<p>E.g.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>struct details_status {
    char a;
    int b;
};

struct details_status_packed {
    struct details_status inner;
} __attribute__((packed));

struct details_state {
    union {
        struct details_status        D1;
        struct details_status_packed D1_packed;
    };

    union {
        struct details_status        D2;
        struct details_status_packed D2_packed;
    };
};

struct details {
    struct details_state        details_states[2];
} __attribute__((packed));

void print_details_status(struct details_status_packed *s) {
    printf(&quot;a=%u, b=%u\n&quot;, s-&gt;inner.a, s-&gt;inner.b);
}

void foo(struct details *details) {
    print_details_status(&amp;details-&gt;details_states[0].D1_packed);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: there might be the temptation to use the <code>__aligned__</code> attribute:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>void print_details_status(
    struct details_status __attribute__((__aligned__(1))) *s);
</code></pre>
<p>Unfortunately, this does not work because gcc does not allow to lower alignment for structs and <code>__packed__</code> is not allowed at this place.</p>
